WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.867
The defense rests in

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:02.369
the Alex Murdaugh murder trial.

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:03.370
Attorneys for Murdaugh.

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:04.037
What's prominent

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:05.605
attorney accused of killing his wife

00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:06.039
and son

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:08.174
called their final witnesses today,

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:09.542
including Murdaugh's brother

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:10.777
broke down while describing

00:00:10.777 --> 00:00:12.278
how he cleaned up the blood soaked

00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:14.280
crime scene once police left.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.350
Diane Gallagher is OUTFRONT

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:19.719
Thanks for your honor.

00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:20.520
The defense

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.055
resting its case in the Alex

00:00:22.055 --> 00:00:23.790
Murdaugh double murder trial

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.859
with its final witnesses today.

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:26.860
The defense

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.628
calling to experts to the stand,

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:29.729
trying to poke holes

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:31.264
and create doubt around the state's

00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:32.866
timeline of what happened

00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:34.200
the night of June seven

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.502
when Maggie and Paul Murdaugh

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:36.436
were killed.

00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:37.404
A pathologist

00:00:37.404 --> 00:00:38.972
questioning how the time of death

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:40.273
was initially calculated

00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:41.341
by the county coroner.

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:41.941
By far,

00:00:41.941 --> 00:00:44.110
the best, best methodology

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:45.779
is to do a core body temperature

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:46.980
and an ambient temperature

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.181
and an ambient temperature. Yes.

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:50.016
So tell me what you would learn

00:00:50.016 --> 00:00:51.151
by sticking your hand

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:53.286
under the armpit of a deceased.

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:54.487
You wouldn't learn anything

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:55.688
of that impression.

00:00:55.688 --> 00:00:57.590
And a crime scene analyst,

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.259
the individual who shot first

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:02.462
with the shotgun man, Emily was stunned.

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:04.931
Probably bloody material in his eyes.

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:06.232
And maybe he'd been injured

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:09.135
and would have taken some degree of time

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:10.470
to recover.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.372
Once again, floating the defenses

00:01:12.372 --> 00:01:13.873
to shooter theory.

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:15.008
To me, there's

00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:16.309
structurally difficult

00:01:16.309 --> 00:01:18.678
for the same shooter to have two arms

00:01:18.678 --> 00:01:20.146
and no practical reason

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:21.314
for that to happen.

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:23.183
Add that to what I believe happened

00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:24.017
to the shooter

00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:26.186
who fired first with the shotgun

00:01:26.186 --> 00:01:27.087
and I think

00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:28.488
it tips in favor

00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:30.924
of the probability of two shooters.

00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:32.792
The state pushing back.

00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:36.362
And you referred to Paul being shot

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:38.431
in the back of the head,

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:40.834
kind of like an explosion.

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:42.469
Correct.

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:44.904
And I'm not a physicist or anything here.

00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:47.740
But when you fire a shotgun,

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:50.343
things are going

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:51.578
in a direction

00:01:51.578 --> 00:01:53.780
that the gun is fired, right? Correct.

00:01:54.314 --> 00:01:56.382
So there wouldn't be just like a boom

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:58.852
explosion, like from a bomb.

00:01:59.853 --> 00:02:00.386
Shotgun.

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:00.753
Actually,

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:02.222
that's more exactly

00:02:02.222 --> 00:02:03.656
what it would be like. Yes.

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:04.858
I read the whole

00:02:04.858 --> 00:02:05.825
closing out the day

00:02:05.825 --> 00:02:07.427
and the defense's case,

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:08.962
emotional testimony

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:10.263
from the final witness,

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:11.898
Alex Murdaugh's younger brother,

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:13.266
John Marvin Murdaugh,

00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:14.100
who testified

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.502
about cleaning the crime scene

00:02:15.502 --> 00:02:16.603
the next day.

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:18.271
Y'all can imagine what I experienced.

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:20.406
It had not been cleaned up as cold

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:21.908
blood as old brains are.

00:02:21.908 --> 00:02:23.743
So pieces of skull

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:24.277
or in

00:02:24.277 --> 00:02:25.145
when I say brains,

00:02:25.145 --> 00:02:27.580
it could just be tissue.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:28.681
I don't know what it was.

00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:30.116
It was just it was terrible.

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:31.618
But during cross-examination,

00:02:31.618 --> 00:02:32.952
the prosecution called back

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:34.921
to Alex Murdaugh's own testimony

00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:36.156
when he admitted to lying

00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:37.423
about being at the kennels

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:39.259
before the murders happened there.

00:02:39.259 --> 00:02:39.759
Would you agree

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:42.529
that that is not full cooperation by him

00:02:42.529 --> 00:02:44.364
not telling SLED

00:02:44.364 --> 00:02:45.932
that he was at the kennel? Correct.

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:48.101
I would say that, yes, he lied

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:52.238
Now, the jury is actually going

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:54.007
to be leaving the courtroom,

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:54.674
taking a trip

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:56.676
to Moselle, the Murdaugh family property

00:02:56.843 --> 00:02:58.444
where the murders happened.

00:02:58.444 --> 00:02:59.479
The judge granted

00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:00.780
the defense's request

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.515
for the visit today.

00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:04.150
But before any of that happens,

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:05.985
the state still has its rebuttal

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:07.320
period, Erin.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:08.021
The prosecutor

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:09.122
saying their plan to call four

00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:11.357
to five witnesses tomorrow.

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:13.159
All right, Diane, thank you very much.

00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:14.294
She's been covering this trial from

00:03:14.294 --> 00:03:15.028
the very beginning.

